  "response": "여기 앉아서 \"그래서 뭐가 문제인데\"라는 생각이 올라온다고 하셨잖아요. 그런데 아까 말씀하신 내용만 들어도, 며칠 잠을 설치고, 자꾸 먹게 되고, 그 모습이 무서웠다고 하셨는데—그걸 \"문제가 아니다\"라고 스스로에게 말하고 계신 거 같아요.\n\n그리고 그 동기가 던진 말도 그렇고, 임원들 반응도 그렇고, 외부에서 뭔가 평가를 받으면 \"내가 뭘 위해 사는지\"까지 흔들리는 느낌이 드는 것 같은데, 맞을까요?",
  "reasoning": "Let me analyze this conversation carefully.\n\nThe client is sharing several things:\n1. They feel pressure to \"organize\" their thoughts before speaking, even in therapy - they feel they need to present something coherent.\n2. A close colleague asked \"what are you even living for?\" - this shook them deeply, causing insomnia.\n3. They had an internal presentation where executives had a lukewarm response - this preceded the sleep issues and overeating.\n4. They feel scared of their own reaction pattern (insomnia, overeating) because \"this isn't who I am.\"\n5. Now sitting here, they're again questioning \"so what's the problem?\" - which is the same pattern of invalidating their own experience.\n\nKey observations:\n- There's a meta-pattern here: the client is invalidating their own distress in real-time. They came here, and immediately started questioning whether they have a right to be here or whether their problem is \"real enough.\"\n- The colleague's question \"what are you living for?\" seems to have landed on a sore spot - possibly touching on something about meaning/purpose that was already fragile.\n- The \"I'm not usually like this\" + fear of their own reaction suggests they're judging themselves harshly for struggling.\n- The pressure to \"organize\" before speaking mirrors perhaps a pattern of needing to justify or prove their experience is valid before sharing it.\n\nWhat would be most helpful right now?\n- I should NOT just validate or ask another question.\n- I should gently point out the meta-pattern I'm noticing: the way they're questioning their own right to feel what they feel, both in life and right now in this room. This could be genuinely illuminating.\n- I want to be careful not to be too interpretive or clinical-sounding.\n- I should acknowledge what they've shared without being overly effusive.\n- The \"그래서 뭐가 문제인데\" thought is actually important material - it's not just noise, it's part of the problem they're describing.\n\nI'll reflect the pattern of self-questioning/self-invalidating that's happening both in their daily life and right now, and gently offer that this pattern itself might be worth looking at. I'll keep it natural and not too long.\n",
